---
number: 693
title: All Life Doth Like a Summer’s Day
author: Anonymous
composer: W. H. Doane
sp_category: "46"
topic: Funeral
notes: "AUTO: chorus detected heuristically — verify"
---

[verse 1]
All life doth like a summer’s day
Its light and shadow see;
And e’en the longest happy day
Can only fleeting be.

[chorus]
O how soon the time doth flee,
That the Lord gave to me;
What so withered is at night
In the morn was fresh and bright.

[verse 2]
So must this fleeting life of ours
A time for sowing be,
A gracious time for planting flow’rs
For all eternity.

[chorus]
O how soon the time doth flee,
That the Lord gave to me;
What so withered is at night
In the morn was fresh and bright.

[verse 3]
Come, early do thou now awake
And labor while you may,
For all too soon the night will break
Your work on earth to stay.

[chorus]
O how soon the time doth flee,
That the Lord gave to me;
What so withered is at night
In the morn was fresh and bright.
